## Pareto Front Analysis

Focusing on experiments with both Elastic Modulus and Pore Fraction measured, using the **current bath context (PolarClean 10 wt%)** only for the most recent experiment:

**Key observations from the data:**

The tension between objectives is clear: high modulus tends to correlate with low pore fraction (dense polymer network), while high pore fraction tends to correlate with lower modulus. The Pareto-relevant candidates are:

| Condition | Modulus (bar) | Pore Fraction | Bath | Quality |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 14-0add-8deg-N2-1250s (run1) | 103.6 | 0.75 | 0 wt% | minor creases |
| 14p8-17deg-N2-1425s (run1) | 181.4 | 0.65 | 0 wt% | clean |
| 14p8-17deg-N2-1425s (run2) | 217.0 | 0.82 | 0 wt% | wrinkles |
| 14p8-17deg-N2-1425s (run4) | 259.7 | 0.44 | 0 wt% | clean |
| 15-9deg-N2-1600s (run1) | 246.6 | 0.77 | 0 wt% | clean |
| 15-9deg-N2-1600s (run4) | 221.3 | 0.75 | 0 wt% | top gap |
| 15-9deg-N2-1600s (run5) | 276.8 | 0.85 | 0 wt% | top gap |
| 15-9deg-N2-1600s (run8) | 211.1 | 0.68 | 0 wt% | clean |
| 14p5-10deg-N2-1500s (run1) | 222.3 | 0.67 | 0 wt% | clean |
| 14p5-10deg-N2-1500s (run2) | 209.1 | 0.69 | 0 wt% | clean |
| 14p5-10deg-N2-1500s (run4) | 269.5 | 0.65 | 0 wt% | clean |
| 15-9deg-PolarClean-10wt | 124.3 | 0.55 | 10 wt% | clean |
| 14p5-10deg-PolarClean-10wt | 132.7 | 0.62 | 10 wt% | clean |

**The 10 wt% PolarClean bath clearly reduces modulus substantially** compared to 0 wt% bath at similar polymer/process conditions. The first 10 wt% bath experiment (15-0add-9deg-250s-N2-1600s) gave modulus=124 bar, pore fraction=0.55 — well below the Pareto front established with 0 wt% bath.

**Pareto-dominant candidates in the current 10 wt% bath context:**
- The 14.5 wt% polymer / 10°C / 200s / 10 wt% bath gave modulus=132.7, pore fraction=0.62 — the best so far under the current bath
- The 15 wt% / 9°C / 250s / 10 wt% bath gave modulus=124.3, pore fraction=0.55

**What's needed:** We need to understand whether the 10 wt% PolarClean bath can achieve higher modulus while maintaining high pore fraction. The 0 wt% bath data shows that polymer_wt ~14–15 wt%, bath_temp ~8–9°C, moderate wait times (~200–300s), and moderate pullcast speed (~6–8 mm/s) with N2 can yield pore fractions of 0.65–0.77 with moduli of 200–270 bar. The 10 wt% bath appears to soften the modulus significantly.

**Key gap:** We have only 3 data points with the 10 wt% PolarClean bath (one failed entirely). We need more exploration to understand the 10 wt% bath Pareto frontier. The most informative next step is to try a higher polymer concentration (e.g., 15.5–16 wt%) with the 10 wt% bath, which in the 0 wt% bath context tended to give higher modulus. The 16 wt% / 9°C / 250s / 10 wt% bath gave modulus=176.7, pore fraction=0.52 — higher modulus but lower pore fraction.

**Proposed direction:** Try polymer_wt=15.0 with slightly lower bath temp (8°C instead of 9°C) and longer wait time (300s) to promote more phase separation and potentially higher modulus while maintaining pore fraction. This is a modest perturbation from the best current 10 wt% bath result (14.5/10°C/200s → modulus=132.7, pore fraction=0.62), moving toward conditions that in the 0 wt% bath gave higher pore fractions (the 15/8°C/250s region gave pore fractions of 0.58–0.68 with moduli of 196–265 bar).

Alternatively, the 14.5 wt% / 10°C / 200s / 10 wt% bath result (modulus=132.7, pore fraction=0.62) has only one clean measurement — it warrants a repeat to confirm. However, given the modest absolute values and that we have only 3 10 wt% bath points total, **exploration is more valuable** than repeating a mediocre result.

