## Pareto Front Analysis

Focusing on experiments with both Elastic Modulus and Pore Fraction measured, using the **current bath context (PolarClean 10 wt%)** for the most recent experiments:

**Key observations from the 10 wt% PolarClean bath experiments:**
- `15-0add-9deg-bathSolv-PolarClean-10wt-250s-N2-1600s`: E=124 bar, PF=0.55 (good quality)
- `14p5-0add-10deg-bathSolv-PolarClean-10wt-200s-N2-1500s` (run1): E=133 bar, PF=0.62 (good quality)
- `14p5-0add-10deg-bathSolv-PolarClean-10wt-200s-N2-1500s` (run2): E=105 bar, PF=0.66 (good quality)
- `15p5-0add-9deg-bathSolv-PolarClean-10wt-250s-N2-1600s_run2`: E=111 bar, PF=0.59 (good quality)
- `15-0add-8deg-bathSolv-PolarClean-10wt-300s-N2-1600s`: E=147 bar, PF=0.58 (top gap defect)

The 10 wt% PolarClean bath clearly **reduces modulus** compared to 0 wt% bath at similar conditions (e.g., 15wt% polymer, bath ~9°C, 0 wt% bath gives E~200-280 bar, PF~0.63-0.77). The bath solvent slows phase separation, yielding softer, more open structures.

**Best Pareto candidates in the current bath (10 wt% PolarClean):**
- High pore fraction: run2 of 14.5/10°C/7mm/200s gives PF=0.66, E=105 bar
- Higher modulus: 15wt%/8°C/300s gives E=147 bar, PF=0.58

**From 0 wt% bath (historical reference for structure):**
- The 14-0add-8deg/120s/N2/1250s condition gave PF=0.75 (run1), 0.71 (run2), 0.63 (run3), 0.57 (run4) with E~103-246 bar — high pore fraction but variable
- The 15-0add-9deg/250s/N2/1600s condition (0 wt% bath) gave PF up to 0.85 with E~200-315 bar

**Key insight:** In the 10 wt% bath, we've only tested polymer_wt ≤ 15.5. Higher polymer concentration (e.g., 16–17 wt%) with the 10 wt% bath is unexplored. From 0 wt% bath data, higher polymer_wt tends to increase modulus. The 10 wt% bath may preserve higher pore fraction by slowing phase separation. This is a promising unexplored region.

**Validation status:** The 14.5 wt%/10°C/7mm/200s/1500s condition in the 10 wt% bath has two runs (run1: E=133, PF=0.62; run2: E=105, PF=0.66) — both good quality, showing moderate reproducibility. The 15 wt%/9°C/6mm/250s/1600s in 10 wt% bath has only one valid run (run1 good, run2 failed). These are not yet confirmed Pareto leaders.

**Recommendation:** Explore a modestly higher polymer concentration (15.5 wt%) with the 10 wt% bath at conditions similar to the best-performing region (bath ~9°C, moderate wait time ~250s, moderate speed ~7 mm/s). This is distinct from the already-tested 15.5/9°C/6mm/250s and 15.5/9°C/7mm/250s points. I'll use bath_temp=8°C (slightly colder to boost modulus) and pullcast_speed=8 mm/s (slightly faster, undersampled in this region), coupon_to_bath_wait_time=200s, nips_bath_wait_time=1600s. This explores a new corner of the 10 wt% bath space that could yield both higher modulus (from higher polymer_wt + colder bath) and maintained pore fraction.

