**Rationale:**
- **polymer_wt = 15.5**: Slightly higher than the 14.5–15 wt% cluster tested in the 10 wt% bath; from 0 wt% bath data, 15–16 wt% polymer tends to increase modulus while the 10 wt% bath may preserve higher pore fraction vs. 0 wt% bath
- **bath_temp = 8°C**: Colder than the 9°C cluster; from 0 wt% bath data, lower bath temp tends to increase modulus; only one 10 wt% bath point at 8°C (15 wt%/300s, top-gap defect) — this is a new combination
- **pullcast_speed = 8 mm/s**: Undersampled in the 10 wt% bath region (most points use 6–7 mm/s); slightly faster casting may improve coverage and reduce wrinkles
- **coupon_to_bath_wait_time = 200s**: Consistent with the productive region; moderate evaporation time
- **mixing_temp = 45°C**: Appropriate for 15.5 wt% (requires mixing, not at stock concentration)
- **Expected benefit**: Potential Pareto improvement — higher modulus from increased polymer_wt + colder bath, while the 10 wt% bath solvent may maintain or improve pore fraction relative to 0 wt% bath at similar conditions; this fills a genuine gap in the 10 wt% bath exploration

