[2026-10-24 12:06:27] LLM suggestion: ## Analysis

### Pareto Front Assessment (current bath: PolarClean 10 wt%)

The current bath context is **PolarClean 10 wt%**. Only one experiment was run under this exact bath condition with valid measurements:

- **15-0add-9deg-PolarClean-10wt-250s-N2-1600s**: Elastic Modulus = 124 bar, Pore Fraction = 0.55 (good quality, defect-free)

All other historical data used a 0 wt% bath. Those results cannot be directly compared for Pareto purposes, but they inform what parameter regimes produce high modulus and high pore fraction.

### Key observations from 0 wt% bath history (for parameter guidance):

Looking at the best two-objective candidates from 0 wt% bath:
- **14-0add-8deg-120s-N2-1250s** (runs 1–4): Modulus ~103–246 bar, Pore Fraction ~0.57–0.75 — the highest pore fractions observed anywhere
- **14p8-0add-17deg-530s-N2-1425s** (run 2): Modulus 217 bar, Pore Fraction **0.82** — highest pore fraction in dataset, but with wrinkles
- **15-0add-9deg-250s-N2-1600s** (runs 1–10): Modulus ~177–315 bar, Pore Fraction ~0.51–0.85 — very promising range, multiple runs
- **14p5-0add-10deg-200s-N2-1500s** (runs 1–4): Modulus ~209–269 bar, Pore Fraction ~0.65–0.69 — consistently high pore fraction with moderate-high modulus

### Current bath (PolarClean 10 wt%) observations:

The 10 wt% PolarClean bath slows phase separation (solvent in bath reduces driving force), which in 0 wt% comparisons tends to produce lower modulus but can maintain or increase pore fraction. The one valid 10 wt% result (15 wt% polymer, 9°C bath, 250s wait, speed 6) gave modulus 124 bar and pore fraction 0.55 — lower modulus than the 0 wt% equivalent (~200–277 bar), but pore fraction is similar.

### Strategy

The 10 wt% bath is new territory. We need more data points to map the Pareto front under this bath. The most informative next experiment should:
1. Use a parameter regime that produced **high pore fraction AND reasonable modulus** in the 0 wt% bath
2. Differ meaningfully from the existing 10 wt% data points to build coverage
3. Avoid near-duplicates of already-tested 10 wt% conditions

The **14.5 wt% polymer, bath 10°C, 200s wait, speed 7** condition gave modulus ~132 bar and pore fraction 0.62–0.66 under 10 wt% bath (two runs). The **15.5 wt% polymer** at similar conditions gave 110 bar / 0.59 (run 2) — lower modulus.

An under-explored region in the 10 wt% bath: **higher polymer concentration (15–15.5 wt%)** with **lower bath temperature (8°C)** and **moderate wait time (~200–250s)**. In the 0 wt% bath, 15 wt% polymer at 8°C bath gave modulus ~190–252 bar and pore fraction ~0.53–0.69. Under the 10 wt% bath, the slower phase separation may shift toward higher pore fraction. The 15.5 wt% / 8°C / 200s / speed 8 condition (run 1) failed (thickness ~0.5 µm — membrane dissolved/lost). The 15 wt% / 8°C / 300s / speed 6 gave modulus 147 bar / pore fraction 0.58 — decent but one data point.

A good exploration target: **polymer_wt = 15.0, bath_temp = 8°C, pullcast_speed = 5 mm/s, coupon_to_bath_wait_time = 200s, mixing_temp = 40°C, N2 = true, nips_bath_wait_time = 1600s**. This is distinct from the existing 10 wt% bath points (which used speed 6–8 or 7), uses a slightly slower cast speed to reduce wrinkling risk, and targets the moderate-polymer/cold-bath regime that showed high pore fractions in 0 wt% bath. It will tell us whether the 10 wt% bath can support both objectives simultaneously at this polymer concentration.

